                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | High-energy dog does NOT have to mean high-energy house. Gabby is 21 weeks old—a Belgian Malinois puppy with exactly the kind of energy you'd expect. And she LOVES that Frisbee. Outside? Run. Jump. Chase. Catch. Get crazy. Have fun. But watch what happens when we come inside. Our 9-month-old granddaughter is sitting on the floor playing with Gabby's favorite Frisbee. Gabby isn't grabbing it, pestering her, bouncing around her, or demanding it back. She's simply calm, gentle, and letting the baby have it. Same dog. Same Frisbee. Different rules. That's intentional. From the beginning, one of our biggest priorities with Gabby wasn't teaching commands—it was teaching habits and manners we actually want to live with. We haven't tried to take the Malinois out of her. Quite the opposite. We give her appropriate places to use all that drive and energy. But we also teach her that calm is a behavior. Inside is different from outside. Being around a baby is different from playing Frisbee in the yard. Dogs become good at what they repeatedly practice, which means we have to be intentional about the behaviors we teach AND the habits we allow. Don't just ask: “How do I stop my dog from doing this?” Start asking: “What do I want my dog to do instead?” Then practice THAT until it becomes normal. 🐾 Habits • Obedience • Manners • Engagement Teach the habits you want to live with.+| 
