                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Socializing your puppy does NOT mean they need to meet everyone. Gabby turned 5 months old the day I filmed this. About 20 feet behind us, a contractor is wearing a reflective vest and running a loud gas-powered post-hole auger. And that's exactly why I brought her outside to train. I don't want Gabby believing every person who enters our property is her business. I don't want every noise, dog, squirrel, or unfamiliar object demanding her attention. I want her to learn neutrality. Listen to the video. That auger is LOUD. Yet Gabby thinks we're just playing. What she doesn't know is that inside that game we're practicing recall, sit, down, fetch, retrieve, Drop It, impulse control—and most importantly, ENGAGEMENT. She's excited. She's highly aroused. And I'm teaching her that she can still think, listen, and communicate with me. That's real-world puppy socialization. Sometimes socialization isn't teaching your puppy to interact with something. It's teaching them that something can exist without involving them. I'll let Gabby know when it's time to say hello. I'll let her know when it's time to play. But when we're working together, everything else should start becoming background noise. The world doesn't have to disappear. It just has to become background noise. 🐾 HOME Dog Training Habits • Obedience • Manners • Engagement                                          +| 
